# Commit conventions

This project uses **Nx Release** (`scripts/release.mjs`) with **conventional commits**
(`@commitlint/config-conventional`). Every public package is released under one shared version. Every commit message
must follow the format below so releases are automated and issues are auto-closed when the PR merges.

## Format

```
<type>(<scope>): <subject>

<body>

<footer>
```

### Types (from `release` in `nx.json`)

A commit only counts when it changes files inside a released package.

| Type                | Release  | When to use                  |
| ------------------- | -------- | ---------------------------- |
| `feat`              | minor    | New feature / capability     |
| `fix`               | patch    | Bug fix                      |
| `perf`              | patch    | Performance improvement      |
| `revert`            | patch    | Reverting an earlier commit  |
| `refactor`          | **none** | Code change, same behavior   |
| `test`              | **none** | Adding / updating tests only |
| `docs`              | **none** | Documentation only           |
| `chore`, `ci`       | **none** | Build, CI, tooling           |

### Documentation changes — use `docs`, not `fix`

Changes inside the `documentation/` directory (docs site code, MDX files, doc components, etc.) must use the `docs`
type, even when fixing a bug in documentation code. Using `fix` would trigger a patch release and bump package versions,
which is wrong for doc-only changes. Reserve `fix` for bugs in published library packages.

### BREAKING CHANGE

Append `!` after type or add `BREAKING CHANGE:` in the footer → **major** release.

## Issue references — auto-close on merge

GitHub resolves issues listed with
[closing keywords](https://docs.github.com/en/issues/tracking-your-work-with-issues/using-issues/linking-a-pull-request-to-an-issue)
in the commit body or footer. **Every bug-fix commit must reference all related issue numbers** (primary + duplicates
from `docs/bugs/*.md`) so they auto-close when the commit reaches the default branch.

### Rules

1. **Footer** — Use `Closes #NNN` (or `Fixes #NNN`) for the primary issue. Add every deduped issue on the same line or
   subsequent lines:

   ```
   fix(pan): stop velocity from being disabled at scale <= 1

   The velocity calculation guard rejected all scales not strictly greater
   than 1, preventing inertia at the default zoom level.

   Closes #363
   Closes #433
   ```

2. **Multiple issues in one commit** — List each on its own `Closes` line. Do **not** comma-separate them
   (`Closes #1, #2` only closes #1 on some parsers).

3. **Feature requests** — Use `Closes` when the commit fully implements the request. Use `Ref #NNN` (no closing keyword)
   when partially addressed.

4. **Regression test commits** — Reference the issue but do **not** close it (the fix commit closes it):

   ```
   test(pan): add regression for velocity at scale <= 1

   Ref #363
   ```

5. **Scope** — Use the library area: `pan`, `zoom`, `pinch`, `bounds`, `controls`, `animation`, `css`, `build`, `api`,
   `minimap`.

## Examples

```
fix(zoom): fire onZoom callbacks from programmatic zoomIn/zoomOut

Programmatic methods now emit the same callback sequence as user-driven
gestures.

Closes #369
Closes #259
Closes #305
```

```
feat(controls): add directional pan buttons to Controls component

Closes #527
Closes #326
```

```
fix(build): include src/ in published package for source maps

Closes #529
Closes #542
```
